@misc{Montebugnoli_24269,
 abstract = {Between March 1800 and early 1801, in the wake of the publication of the orchestral score of Haydn’s oratorio Die Schöpfung Hob. XXI:2, no less than seven distinct piano reductions appeared from the most important European music publishers, some realized by well-known musicians: August Eberhard Müller, Sigismond von Neukomm, Muzio Clementi, and Daniel Steibelt. If their existence again confirms the status of piano arrangements as indispensable tools for dissemination of the orchestral repertoire, a closer analysis reveals fundamentally different approaches in both conception and execution.
How should we interpret the diversity of arrangers’ solutions? To what extent are these related to different piano engineering? What insights, in turn, can the study of these arrangements provide into the musical and pianistic cultures to which they belong? To answer these questions, I will focus on “Die Vorstellung des Chaos,” the opening tableau of Haydn’s oratorio. I will first identify and compare the strategies employed by the arrangers. Then, through performances on different historical pianos of the most representative versions, I will illustrate how the narrative of the piece can be reshaped by arranger’s choices.
Finally, I will show how the study of these sources can guide a historically informed reactualization of piano arranging practice by performing my own arrangement of the fourth movement of Beethoven’s Eroica Symphony Op. 55, that I originally designed for a copy of the 1803 Erard piano that Beethoven received during the composition of the work. I will adapt the arrangement for the 1798 Broadwood piano, drawing on Clementi’s Creation arrangement.
},
